<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carcassonne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carcassonne is a city in the Aude <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on the route between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. Its fortified upper town, the Cité, with a double ring of walls and 52 towers, was the seat of counts and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trencavel">Trencavel</a> viscounts, whose surrender to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> in 1209 ended their rule. Restored by Viollet-le-Duc in the 19th century, it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carcassonne.jpg" alt="Carcassonne" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chensiyuan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_carcassonne_aerial_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
